<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Chopin returns three days after his first visit, masked and seen in an airborne infection isolation room, for his results; Dr. Kowalczyk-Grant of infectious diseases sees him the same morning. He has stayed at home with no students or visitors, and his lessons have moved online. His cough and night sweats are unchanged, and he has coughed up streaks of blood once since the first visit. His three sputum smears show acid-fast bacilli (2+, 2+, and 1+), and the nucleic acid amplification test on the first sample detected Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex with no rifampin resistance; molecular tests for isoniazid and fluoroquinolone resistance and the cultures are pending. The interferon-gamma release assay is positive, HIV is negative, hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody are negative with surface antibody positive from vaccination, and hepatitis C antibody is negative. He was relieved to have a name for the illness and asked how long treatment lasts and when he can teach again. After the four-month and six-month regimens were explained, he chose the four-month regimen and took his first observed dose with a meal in the clinic. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Results</td><td>Smears 2+, 2+, 1+; amplification test positive without rifampin resistance; IGRA positive; HIV negative; hepatitis B immune; hepatitis C negative</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Cough, one more streak of blood, night sweats</td></tr><tr><td>Decision</td><td>Four-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen, first dose today</td></tr><tr><td>Isolation</td><td>At home; lessons online</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

