<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pulmonary tuberculosis, cavitary, smear-positive (A15.0)</content>. Confirmed today, replacing the presenting problem of cough, hemoptysis, and weight loss. No rifampin resistance. He meets the criteria for the four-month regimen: over 12 years old, weight 48.4 kg (at least 40 kg), pulmonary disease only, HIV negative, no prolonged QT history and no QT-prolonging or interacting medicines, normal potassium (4.0), magnesium (2.0), liver tests, and creatinine, no earlier tuberculosis treatment, and specimens sent for susceptibility testing before the first dose. Dr. Kowalczyk-Grant agrees. [S]<list><item>Rifapentine 1200 mg, moxifloxacin 400 mg, isoniazid 300 mg, and pyrazinamide 1000 mg daily with food for eight weeks, then rifapentine, moxifloxacin, and isoniazid for nine weeks, with pyridoxine 50 mg, by video-observed therapy seven days a week.</item><item>Three sputum smears in the third week for the release decision; then monthly sputum until two cultures are negative.</item><item>Liver tests in two to three weeks because of his low weight and albumin; stop the doses and call the same day for yellow eyes, dark urine, vomiting, or abdominal pain.</item><item>Stop moxifloxacin and call for tendon pain; report numbness or tingling in his fingers.</item><item>No mineral supplements, antacids, or zinc products within four hours before or eight hours after the dose.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Moderate protein-calorie malnutrition (E44.0)</content>. BMI 16.7 today. Appetite should return as the infection comes under control. [S]<list><item>First dietitian visit by video this week; supplement drinks in the afternoon and evening, well away from the morning dose.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Symptom review: cough, sputum, hemoptysis, fever, night sweats</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Cough, one more streak of blood, night sweats; no improvement yet before treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, with the pyrazinamide dose checked against the weight band</td><td>Monthly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>48.4 kg: pyrazinamide 1000 mg for the 40 to under 55 kg band</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects: hepatitis symptoms, tendon pain, numbness or tingling, rash, nausea, orange body fluids</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline tendon and nerve examination normal; warning symptoms explained</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (ALT, AST, bilirubin)</td><td>Baseline; repeat for symptoms or risk factors (here at two to three weeks, for low weight and albumin)</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline ALT 18, AST 24, bilirubin 0.5, all normal; repeat ordered for the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium, calcium, magnesium, platelets, creatinine</td><td>Baseline, then as indicated</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline potassium 4.0, calcium 9.0, magnesium 2.0, platelets 442 (raised with inflammation), creatinine 0.78</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Electrocardiogram (QT interval)</td><td>Not routine; only if clinically indicated</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No QT history, no QT-prolonging medicines, normal potassium and magnesium</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sputum acid-fast smear and culture</td><td>Baseline, then monthly until two consecutive cultures are negative</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three baseline smears 2+, 2+, 1+; cultures pending</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Nucleic acid amplification test with rifampin resistance</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex detected; rifampin resistance not detected</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Rapid molecular testing for isoniazid and fluoroquinolone resistance</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Sent on the first sample; pending</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Phenotypic susceptibility (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, moxifloxacin)</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Ordered</td><td>On the culture isolate when it grows</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Interferon-gamma release assay</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Positive; supports, but could not by itself prove or exclude, active tuberculosis</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>HIV test</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>HIV-1/2 antigen and antibody negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis B and C serology</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody negative, surface antibody positive (vaccinated); hepatitis C antibody negative</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Chest X-ray</td><td>Baseline; at two months only if baseline cultures were negative; end of treatment optional</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Right upper lobe cavity on X-ray and CT at the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Doses observed and the completion clock (56 doses within 70 days, 63 within 84 days, 119 within five months)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Video-observed therapy set up with the health department; first dose observed in clinic today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Drug interactions: rifapentine, and mineral products with moxifloxacin</td><td>With every new medicine or supplement</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No other medicines; mineral products to be kept apart from moxifloxacin; orange urine and tears expected</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home airborne isolation and the release criteria</td><td>Until three negative smears, clinical improvement, and two weeks of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Continues at home; lessons online</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Report to the health department</td><td>Same day as suspicion and on confirmation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Suspected case reported at the first visit; confirmation sent today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Contact investigation (students, friends, close contacts)</td><td>Led by the health department from diagnosis</td><td>Referred</td><td>Health department listing his students and other close contacts; their results are not part of his record</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At diagnosis, at two months, and at the end of treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>7 at the first visit, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Nutrition: dietitian, intake, supplement drinks</td><td>Every visit until weight is restored</td><td>Ordered</td><td>First dietitian visit by video this week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sweat chloride and serum alpha-1 antitrypsin</td><td>Once, to address the alternatives</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Advised by infectious diseases; at the next visit, once he is out of isolation</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Infectious diseases review</td><td>At diagnosis, at two months, and at the end of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today by Dr. Kowalczyk-Grant</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Complete all 119 doses within five months by video-observed therapy. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>Sputum culture negative at two months. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>Return to teaching once the health department confirms he is no longer infectious. In progress; target 1838-04-07 [S]</item><item>Weight back to at least 55 kg by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 5 by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); Interim Guidance: 4-Month Rifapentine-Moxifloxacin Regimen for the Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Pulmonary Tuberculosis - United States, 2022 (2022); An official ATS statement: hepatotoxicity of antituberculosis therapy (2006); Guidelines for the investigation of contacts of persons with infectious tuberculosis: recommendations from the National Tuberculosis Controllers Association and CDC (2005); Diagnosis of Cystic Fibrosis: Consensus Guidelines from the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation (2017); American Thoracic Society/European Respiratory Society statement: standards for the diagnosis and management of individuals with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (2003); Consensus statement: Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition: characteristics recommended for the identification and documentation of adult malnutrition (undernutrition) (2012); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Release smears in the third week, then review at about two and a half weeks with liver tests, the sweat chloride test, and the alpha-1 antitrypsin level. Call the same day for hepatitis symptoms or tendon pain. Next scheduled visit 1838-04-07: two-and-a-half-week review: release from isolation, return to teaching, liver tests, and tests for cystic fibrosis and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
